title: Text search in the store
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Trigrams give a text search its candidates — by value while a field has few distinct texts, by metarecord and built by a fold once it has many — and a page without a count checks the text only where the sort looks.

A `matches` (regex) or `osm` leaf on a field with many distinct values — file names —
cannot scan them all at each keystroke. Each field's texts (strings, `tree_ref`
names) are indexed by the trigrams of their lower-cased form. A search's ''required
literals'' — the literal runs every match contains, extracted from the pattern's
syntax tree (a case-insensitive letter counting as the letter), or an `osm` term —
give trigrams, and the trigrams give candidates, which are then matched against the
real predicate. A pattern with no literal of three characters falls back to a scan of
the distinct values, restricted to the candidates of the enclosing `and`. A text over
512 bytes is not split: its id joins the field's long-text set, which every search
checks.

!! Candidates only

A trigram entry only ever names a ''candidate'', and every candidate is checked. One
too many costs a check; one missing would be a wrong answer. So a text that changes
or goes — a tag removed, a file renamed or deleted — leaves its trigrams where they
are: only a text ''coming'' writes. `mf repo reindex` drops what is stale.

This is what makes the index cheap to write. A file's texts hold some twenty
trigrams; kept exact per metarecord, each write touched twenty scattered keys, and
each removal the same twenty again.

!! Two modes per field

A field keeps its trigrams one of two ways ([[Store tables]]): by value in
`index_trigram_texts`, by metarecord in `index_trigram_holders`, the mode and the
field's counts in `index_trigram_state`; the ids of a text too long to split in
`index_long_texts`, those waiting for a fold in `index_trigram_pending`.

''By value'' — how every field starts. A trigram names the ''distinct texts'' that
hold it (their value keys — a position's name being its value). A write adds entries
only when its text is one the field did not hold: tagging a file with an existing tag
writes nothing. A search joins the texts of its trigrams, tests each text once — on
the key itself, no metarecord is read — and unions the holders of the texts that
match, from their postings. The answer is exact, with nothing
pending. The join reads a few keys per text its trigrams share, so when the rarest of
them is common (`file1` among names that all hold `fil`) it costs more than a scan
of the field's texts, which reads each once: it gives up after a quarter of the
field's holders' worth of keys, and the search scans instead — a page without its
count then checks the text in its walk. Before that bound, `file1` on 2 000 short
names read 4 600 keys for its count and 4 800 for a page; it reads under 3 000 and
1 000.

''By metarecord'' — for a field whose texts are mostly new ones, file names first:
by value, each would write its twenty entries. A trigram names the ''ids'' holding
it, in chunked bitmaps, and these are ''not written by a write'': a write only adds
its id to the field's ''pending'' set (one chunk of one bitmap, shared by the
write's other records). A ''fold'' builds the trigrams of the pending ids from their
texts and empties the set. A search's candidates are the intersection of its
trigrams' bitmaps, plus every pending id, each checked on its own rows; when the
pending ids are too many for that (more than a scan of the field's distinct values
would cost, [[Query planner]]), the search scans the values instead — what a field
without trigrams costs.

A field turns by metarecord when its trigrams name too many texts: the by-value
entries divided by the distinct trigrams — both counted in the field's state as they
are written — exceed 16 (`GRAM_VALUES_MAX`, a starting point to be measured). At
that moment no trigram is kept by metarecord yet, so every holder becomes pending;
the by-value entries go at the next fold. A field never turns back.

!! The fold

`KvStore::fold` builds what is pending, and a reindex ends with one, so a reindexed
repository — one converted from the former format included — has nothing pending.
Otherwise the daemon folds once the repository is quiet, one field's pending ids per
step ([[Folding in idle time]]), and `mf repo fold` at once: until then, a field by
metarecord searched after a large write — the names after a first reconcile — checks
its pending ids, or is scanned when they are too many.

`mf repo check` cannot hold the trigrams to a rebuild entry by entry, since stale
ones are allowed: it verifies that no text lacks one — by value, an entry per trigram
of each text; by metarecord, the id in each trigram's bitmap unless it is pending.

''A page without a count checks text lazily.'' A text leaf, or the text conjuncts of
an `and`, are split off the filter: what is evaluated is the candidate bitmap, and the
predicate is checked only on the ids the sort visits, stopping at the page's end. A
search as you type then costs its page — 0.5 to 3 ms on 5 million files, measured —
instead of its whole match set (170 to 530 ms); only a request asking for the total
pays for the whole set.

`tests/kv_text.rs` counts what a write puts in the trigram tables in each mode and holds the
answers to the oracle; `tests/kv_cost.rs`: on a folded repository a literal search
reads the same number of keys as on one eight times larger (it read the whole field
before: 2 004 keys against 16 004).
